Motors: 


A motor is an electric device that transforms electric power into mechanical power. 
The working of the motors depends on the interaction of the field at the stator (stationary part) with the flux generated by the current armature windings at the rotor (rotating part).
Works on the fact that if a current carrying conductor is put into the field and force is applied on the conductor, the force direction of the force can be found by applying Fleming’s right hand rule. 

Rotor:
The rotatory part of a motor is the rotor over which the armature windings are wound and to which the shaft is connected.
Stator:
The stator is the static part of a motor that provides protection to the internal structure of a motor from outer environment conditions and field woundings are wound on the stator.
Bearings:
The rotor is sustained by bearings that permit the rotor to move on its axis. 
The bearings are mounted on the shaft of a motor to provide less friction during its rotation.
There are two types of bearings mostly used:
– Ball bearings: Good at handling radial loads
– Roller bearings: Higher load carrying capacity due to       higher surface area from the cylindrical bearings, used for high load applications. 

Commutators:
Commutators are the most important part of a dc motor as they convert ac voltage to dc voltage. 
They are connected to the shaft of the motor and carbon brushes are connected to them through which output is taken. 


Types of motors:

1. DC Motor:
Convert electrical energy into mechanical energy.
Operates on the principle that when a current carrying conductor is placed in a magnetic field, it experiences a force that causes the conductor to move, which produces rotation in the motor. 
Parts:
Field coil (stator):
Has the coil wounded around it and produces			magnetic fields.
Sub parts:
Yoke: creates magnetic circuit between the poles.
Pole core: serves as the passage for magnetic flux
Pole body:  contains the pole and its body
Field winding: using insulated copper wire, the coil on this pole when excited with direct current, youll get magnetic flux.
End plates: encapsulate the entire motor, providing casing for the internal parts.

Armature:
Rotating part of the motor which generates mechanical energy. 
Commutator:
Periodically reverses the current between the armature and the circuit and produces steady torque.
Brushes:
Carbon brushes, made up of graphite, which supply current to the winding of the armature.

2. Stepper Motor:
Converts electrical input in the form of a series of pulses into discrete angular movements, which are called steps. The conversion is on one to one basis, that is the motor moves through one step for each input pulse.
The stator has the coils of the motor and the rotor carries a set of permanent magnets. 
The drive systems of stepper motors are classified into open-loop and closed-loop drives. The figure below illustrates the block diagram of a modern driving system for a stepper motor in open-loop mode. The input controller consisting of integrated circuits or microprocessors perform various functions such as generating and governing the number of step command pulses and their timings, logic sequencing, etc. The output signals of the input controller are transmitted to the input terminals of a power driver by which the turning on/off of the motor winding is governed. The power driver may be called a motor driver or simply a driver.



The open-loop drive is an economically advantageous driving method and widely accepted in applications of speed arid position controls. However, there are certain limitations to this type of drive. For instance, a stepper motor driven in the open-loop mode may fail to follow a pulse command when the stepping rate is too high or the inertial load is too heavy. Moreover, the motor motion tends to be oscillatory. The performance of a stepper motor can be improved to a great extent by employing position feedback and/or speed feedback to determine the proper phase(s) to be switched at the proper timings. This type of control is called the closed-loop drive. For this, nowadays, a rotor position sensor such as an optical encoder usually coupled to the rotor shaft is used (Fig. below). Closed-loop control not only prevents step failure but it also gives motor motion much quicker and smoother.




Motor drivers:

L298N:
Dual H-Bridge with high power handling. 

Supports two DC Motors or one stepper motor. 
Max V: 46V, Max I: 2A (per channel)(Channel A and B, so total current is 4A). This differs from the peak current, which is around 3A for when the power supply starts. 

Three pin screw terminal, with 2 for power, one of them for the microcontroller and the other for the motor itself. 
VS for the motor, which can range from 5V to 12V and the other VSS for the logic circuitry, 5V to 7V. 
The other pin is a ground pin. 

There is the OUT1 and OUT2, which have connections for Motor A and OUT3 and OUT4, which have connections for Motor B. To this, two DC motors can be connected or one stepper motor, ranging from a rating of 5 to 12V. 

Has direction control pins and enable pins, with IN1 and IN2 for Motor A and IN3 and IN4 for Motor B. The ENA, ENB pins are used to enable or disable the motors, as well as controlling their speed. PWM signals help adjust the speed. 
Do not apply the voltage to VS and VSS simultaneously. 

Used for DC and Stepper motors both. 


TB6600:
Used for stepper motors. Voltage ranges from 9-42 VDC, max output current is 3.5A per phase and 4A as the peak. Clock speed is at 200kHz and the microstep resolution is full, ½, ¼, ⅛, 1/16 and 1/32. 
The “high voltage” section connects to the motor itself, 		which includes the B-,B+, A-, A+, GND and VCC pins. The A+ and A- connect to the Phase A of the stepper motor and Phase B has the B+, B- connections. 
The “signal” section of the motor driver connects to the ENA-, ENA+, DIR-, DIR+, PUL-, PUL+. 
ENA-, ENA+ are used to turn on and off the supply of the motor, DIR-, DIR+ are used for direction, and PUL+,PUL- are used for the amount of pulses for the stepper motor’s steps.


BTN8982TA:
High current half H bridge motor driver, which has a voltage range of 8V to 18V, with a peak of 40V (dump voltage).
Logic level inputs from 3.3V to 5V PWM signals. 
As it is a half bridge, two are needed for the movement of the motor in both directions. 
 






